WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
Why does a big island near a mainland hold more kinds of animals than a small,

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.360
remote one? One process can give both patterns.

3
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.340
It is a balance between two rates.

4
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:17.633
Start with the mainland.

5
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:23.233
It holds a pool of species that could reach the island, P species in all.

6
00:00:24.373 --> 00:00:27.713
The number living on the island is S, its species richness.

7
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:30.897
Now let animals cross the water.

8
00:00:32.197 --> 00:00:37.237
On an empty island, every arrival belongs to a new species, so the count climbs

9
00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:43.277
fast. Immigration, here, means only the arrival of a species not already present.

10
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:48.727
As the island fills, more arrivals belong to species already there.

11
00:00:50.007 --> 00:00:53.927
An arrival of a kind already present changes nothing in the count.

12
00:00:55.307 --> 00:00:58.827
So new arrivals slow, and stop when all P species live there.

13
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.060
Plot that rate of new arrivals against the number of species on the island.

14
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.040
The blue line starts high on an empty island and falls to zero when S reaches P.

15
00:01:11.183 --> 00:01:13.043
Residents also disappear.

16
00:01:14.383 --> 00:01:17.723
This is local extinction, a loss from this island only.

17
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:23.163
Suppose each resident has the same, independent chance of being lost.

18
00:01:24.423 --> 00:01:27.843
Then more residents mean more losses, and the red line rises.

19
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:32.787
Where the blue line meets the red, new arrivals equal losses.

20
00:01:34.147 --> 00:01:40.027
That richness is the equilibrium, S Below it, arrivals win and the count climbs.

21
00:01:41.507 --> 00:01:44.007
Above it, losses win and the count falls back.

22
00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:46.937
At the crossing, nothing stops.

23
00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:51.417
Species keep arriving and keep vanishing, at the same rate.

24
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:54.297
That shared rate is the turnover.

25
00:01:55.637 --> 00:01:59.877
The count settles near S star and wobbles a little, while the list keeps changing.

26
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:05.267
Now push the island farther from the mainland, keeping its size the same.

27
00:02:06.507 --> 00:02:09.467
Which line moves, and which way does the gold point go?

28
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:19.623
Fewer new species reach a distant island at every richness.

29
00:02:20.903 --> 00:02:24.843
So the blue line drops, the crossing slides left, and S star falls.

30
00:02:26.143 --> 00:02:27.983
The far island holds fewer species.

31
00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:32.087
Now keep the distance and shrink the island instead.

32
00:02:33.427 --> 00:02:37.147
A small island loses residents faster at every richness.

33
00:02:38.487 --> 00:02:41.527
The red line rises, and S star moves left again.

34
00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:47.707
So near, large islands hold the most species, and far, small ones the fewest.

35
00:02:48.907 --> 00:02:51.967
One balance of two rates gives both of the old patterns.

36
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.430
Keep the scope. This is a model.

37
00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.130
It leaves out new species forming on the island.

38
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.990
Real curves need not be straight, because species differ and affect one another.

39
00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.930
And it predicts how many species, not which ones.
